The decarburization behaviour of the steel samples with the chemical composition listed in Table  I  was investigated using a controlled laboratory annealing set-up (see Figure  5 ). Specimens measuring 160 mm × 50 mm × 1.5 mm were clamped between two water-cooled copper electrodes, enabling direct electrical Joule heating of the sample to the target soaking temperature. A series of soaking temperatures ( e.g.  750 °C, 800 °C and 850 °C) were applied, while the soaking time was maintained at 50 s for all trials to ensure consistent thermal exposure. The temperature profile with heating and cooling rates is shown in Figure  6 . The atmosphere was adjusted using mixtures of hydrogen and water vapour in nitrogen, allowing systematic variation of the  p H 2 O / p H 2 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
